// Lesson 5.3 — Into the Cave: Scenes, Persistence & The Refactor
// The architectural payoff lesson. The student walks into a cave (the scene
// switch already works, via the Cave Entrance colliders they tripped in 2.1),
// discovers that every stat they own resets, and refactors stats off
// PlayerController into the StatManager singleton that has been an empty stub
// since Unit 2. Then Start becomes the scene the game begins in, with the HUD.
// GROUND TRUTH: Start (build 0) holds the StatManager object, a Player, a Ground
// Quad, Cave Exit, Collision tilemaps - but NO Canvas. Overworld (build 1) is
// the hub: Cave1 Entrance -> Start, Cave2 Entrance -> Platformer. Platformer
// (build 2) has Cave Exit -> Overworld. SceneSwitch.cs is REAL working code.
// StatManager.cs is an EMPTY STUB and has no singleton yet.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>5.3 — Into the Cave: Scenes, Persistence &amp; The Refactor</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You break the door, you walk in — and everything you have built breaks with it. Your coins
    are gone. Your health is back to full. Your axe has vanished. Nothing is wrong with your
    code: you have just left one scene and entered another, and nothing you stored was ever
    built to survive that. Today you fix it properly, and in doing so you meet the most
    important refactor of the year.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Problem: Walk Into the Cave</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Collect a few coins. Take some damage. Pick up the axe. Now walk into one of the cave
        mouths marked on the overworld map.
    </p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Everything you own, gone</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happened the moment you touched the cave entrance? Which scene are you standing in now — check the Hierarchy, not your memory.</li>
            <li>Look at your coins, your health and your axe. What are they now? Write down each one's value before and after.</li>
            <li>Go back through the cave's exit. Are your coins restored, or are they still reset? What does that tell you about where the numbers were living?</li>
            <li>You have been tripping these cave entrances since <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a>, when you gave the Player a collider. What code has been running every time — and why did it start working only then?</li>
            <li>Open <code>Assets/Scripts/Package Scripts/SceneSwitch.cs</code> and read it. Which method loads the scene, and what does it deliberately <em>restore</em> about the player when you arrive?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>This is not a bug — it is the correct behaviour of a scene load.</strong>
        When Unity loads a scene, it destroys everything in the old one and builds the new one
        from its file. Every GameObject, every field, every number you were holding on
        <code>PlayerController</code>: gone, and replaced by whatever the new scene's copy of the
        Player starts with. Your stats were never lost. They were <em>replaced</em>.
        <br><br>
        <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code> already knows this — that is why it carefully saves and
        restores the player's position, facing and animation controller. It is handling the
        things that <em>should</em> reset. Nobody wrote the code for the things that should not.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs: What Actually Survives?</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        First, learn the world you are actually in. This project has three scenes and they are
        already wired to each other:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Scene</th><th>Load index</th><th>What it is</th><th>What is in it</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Start</code></td>
            <td>0</td>
            <td><strong>Where the game begins</strong></td>
            <td>A StatManager object, a Player, ground, the exit to the overworld. <strong>No Canvas</strong> — which is why the HUD has to go here yourself.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Overworld</code></td>
            <td>1</td>
            <td>The top-down hub you have been building in all year</td>
            <td>The Player, your coins, enemies, turrets, the lever — and entrances to both caves. Also an unused Canvas and EventSystem.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Platformer</code></td>
            <td>2</td>
            <td>The second cave</td>
            <td>Its own tilemaps and a Player, plus an exit back to the overworld.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        So the topology is: the game starts in <strong>Start</strong>, its exit takes you to the
        <strong>Overworld</strong>, and two cave mouths lead to <strong>Start</strong> again and
        to the <strong>Platformer</strong> cave. You have been walking this map since 2.1 without
        knowing it.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Three ways to make a number survive</h3>
        <p>All three are real Unity techniques. Compare what each one costs you:</p>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Approach</th><th>How it works</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>A <code>static</code> field</strong></td>
                <td>The field belongs to the <em>class</em>, not to any object, so destroying the objects does not touch it.</td>
                <td>No lifecycle at all — you cannot reset it cleanly, and in the editor it survives between Play sessions, which makes testing lie to you. Nothing owns it.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>A persistent singleton object</strong></td>
                <td>One object in the scene registers itself as <em>the</em> instance and asks Unity not to destroy it when scenes change.</td>
                <td>You must handle the case where a scene loads a <em>second</em> copy of it, and you must decide who is allowed to read it.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>A ScriptableObject asset</strong></td>
                <td>The data lives in a project asset rather than in a scene, so no scene load can ever touch it.</td>
                <td>A new concept, and one more asset to manage. It survives scene loads <em>and</em> Play sessions, which is either the point or a nuisance depending on what you want.</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
        <ol>
            <li>Your stats should reset when you start a new game, and survive a scene change. Which of the three can express both of those, and which one cannot express the first?</li>
            <li>The <code>Start</code> scene already contains a StatManager object. If you return to <code>Start</code> later, what will be there — and what happens if nothing stops it from registering itself a second time?</li>
            <li>Why can position <em>not</em> be handled this way? What is different about position that makes <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code> handle it instead?</li>
            <li>A player's coins are a fact about <em>this run</em>. A high score is a fact about <em>all runs</em>. Which storage would you use for each, and why is the answer different?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: The Refactor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <code>StatManager.cs</code> has been sitting empty in <code>Assets/Scripts</code> since
        Unit 2, doing nothing. You were told then that you would have a real reason to fill it in
        later, and that the reason would come from a scene change rather than from tidiness.
        This is that reason. <strong>Open it and fill it in.</strong>
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: <code>StatManager.cs</code>.</strong>

        <p><strong>Being the one and only:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>How does a script let other scripts reach a single shared instance without searching the scene? What kind of field makes a value belong to the class rather than to an object?</li>
            <li>Which lifecycle method runs <em>before</em> everything else on scene load, and is therefore the right place to establish a singleton?</li>
            <li>What call tells Unity to keep this object alive when a new scene loads?</li>
            <li>When you return to a scene that contains its own copy of this object, you get a second one. What must happen to the newcomer, and how do you detect it?</li>
            <li>What happens on the very first scene load, when there is no instance yet? Describe the two branches your setup code needs.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Holding the stats:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Move across everything you were storing on the player: the coin count, current and maximum health, and the inventory flag. What type is each one, and which should be Inspector-visible?</li>
            <li>What public methods replace the ones you had on <code>PlayerController</code>? Keep the same shape — a signed amount for health — so your existing callers need the smallest possible change.</li>
            <li>Where does the max-health rule you built in <a href="2.3.html">2.3</a> now live? It should move with the field it protects.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Updating the callers — the part people forget:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Every place that used to read or write those fields on the player must now go through the manager. Search your own code for each one. How many files do you touch?</li>
            <li>What guard do the callers need, in case the manager does not exist yet when they run? When could that actually happen?</li>
            <li>Where in the scene hierarchy must the manager live, and <em>why that scene specifically</em>? Look back at the load index in the table above.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>This is a refactor, not a rewrite.</strong> Nothing about what your game
        <em>does</em> should change. Coins still collect, spikes still hurt, the axe still swaps
        your animation. What changes is <em>where the numbers live</em>. That is the entire
        difference, and it is worth naming: you are not adding a feature, you are moving a
        responsibility. It is the same muscle as 5.1's weapon swap, one level up.
    </div>

    <p>
        Test it the only way that matters: collect coins, take damage, grab the axe, then walk
        into a cave. Come back. Everything should still be there. If it is, you have just solved
        a problem that professional developers solve on every project they ever ship.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: The HUD, and Where the Game Begins</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        You have been reading your stats in the Console all year. That was the right tool while
        you were building, but a player cannot see a Console. Now that the numbers live in one
        place that survives everything, they can be displayed properly.
    </p>
    <p>
        There is a design decision hiding in this: <strong>which scene should the game actually
        begin in?</strong> You have been pressing Play with the Overworld open all year. But the
        <code>Start</code> scene is load index 0 — it is the scene the game loads first when it
        is built and run for real.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the HUD.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which scene must the Canvas live in, given that the game begins there? Does a Canvas survive a scene load on its own?</li>
            <li>Look at the Overworld's Hierarchy — there is a Canvas, a text object and an EventSystem sitting unused. You may move them into <code>Start</code> or build fresh ones. Which is less work, and which teaches you more?</li>
            <li>Which UI component displays a line of text, and which one makes it visible on screen?</li>
            <li>How does the HUD get the values it displays? Should it read them every frame, or only when they change — and what does your answer cost?</li>
            <li>Which script should own the reference to the text component: the HUD itself, or the StatManager?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <p>
        When it works, set your Build Settings so the game launches into <code>Start</code>, press
        Play <em>from that scene</em>, and walk your whole loop: out of the cave, into the
        overworld, collect, take damage, come back. The HUD should never once disagree with
        reality.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Persistence Faults</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Walk from <code>Start</code> to the Overworld and back to <code>Start</code>. How many StatManager objects exist now? If you forgot the duplicate guard, what happens to your stats on the second one?</li>
            <li>Remove the "do not destroy" call. Everything looks fine until you change scenes. What exactly resets, and why did the bug hide until then?</li>
            <li>Delete the manager's GameObject from <code>Start</code> but keep the script. What does the first caller that reaches for it do — and is the error loud or silent?</li>
            <li>Put the StatManager object in the <em>Overworld</em> instead of <code>Start</code>. Press Play from <code>Start</code>. What happens, and what does that tell you about load order?</li>
            <li>Give the manager a <code>static</code> coin field <em>as well as</em> an instance field, and update only one of them. Which one does your HUD read, and why is having both a design smell?</li>
            <li>Rename the <code>Platformer</code> scene but leave the string in <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code> alone. Describe every symptom, then find it. How long did the silent-string hunt take?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Persistence design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Design the respawn contract now that you control it: if you die in the cave, where do you wake up, and what do you keep? Write it as a rule a player could understand in one sentence.</li>
            <li>Add a persistent <em>high score</em> that outlives a play session. Which of the three storage approaches needs to change, and why can the singleton no longer do the job alone?</li>
            <li>The manager currently holds coins, health and inventory. At what point does it hold too much? Sketch how you would split it if it grew to ten values, and name the principle you are applying.</li>
            <li>Read your own commit history from 5.1 to now. Which commit made the biggest change to the <em>shape</em> of the code rather than its behaviour? That is the mark of a refactor.</li>
            <li>Commit and push: <code>"5.3: stats persist across scenes + HUD"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain why a scene load destroys your stats, and why that is correct behaviour rather than a bug</li>
            <li>Name all three scenes, their load indices, and how they are wired together</li>
            <li>Compare static fields, a persistent singleton and a ScriptableObject, and defend your choice</li>
            <li>Build a singleton with a setup hook, a persistence call and a duplicate guard</li>
            <li>Explain why the duplicate guard is genuinely necessary in <em>this</em> project</li>
            <li>Describe what a refactor is, and name what changed and what did not</li>
            <li>Put the HUD in the scene where the game begins, and say why there</li>
            <li>Commit and push</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-05/index.html">Unit 5: The Axe &amp; The Cave</a> — the scene-switch reference</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — SceneManager and object lifetime</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="6.1.html">6.1 — Gravity &amp; Jump</a> — Unit 6 begins, and now that you have one object holding all your state, it is finally safe to add physics that could kill you.</p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="5.3" title="5.3 Into the Cave: Scenes, Persistence &amp; The Refactor — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
